hello welcome back well authorities have  warned that the danger is not over yet  after numerous wildfires across the  country on the hottest day on record  well joining us now is dave walton the  deputy chief fire officer at west  yorkshire fire and rescue service um  what is the situation today  today is a very different day from  yesterday in terms of our incident  activity but um certainly here in  yorkshire the moment where i see it's  very muggy it's still about 23 24  degrees it hasn't rained yet in most  areas so the ground is still dry the  crops are still drying and everything is  still ripe for fire to spread if um that  the right conditions exist and then some  ignites from somewhere  and just talk us through what happened  in west yorkshire over the last couple  of days particularly yesterday how  difficult was the situation  i think was extremely difficult and i've  served for over 30 years in the fire  rescue service and yesterday was  certainly the most challenging day that  i've seen both firefighters on the  ground and our flight control staff  taking the 999 calls and the support  staff to just make this place work on a  day-to-day basis we knew it was going to  be hot of course we did we'd seen the  weather warnings then we made our  preparations for it but from about one  o'clock yesterday afternoon when the  calls really started coming in it just  became relentless i think i've used the  word brutal on a couple of occasions  firefighters were going from one  incident to the next to the next to the  next that they weren't getting the  chance to cool down and rest as we would  would only like them to do and certainly  that you know the 999 core handlers in  fire control as well um they were going  from one call to the next and we were  also taking calls for other fire rescue  services because every fire rescue  service around us was busy uh which also  led to the point that we couldn't help  each other as we would ordinarily do as  well you say of course it was a brutal  day that it was relentless um and that  obviously you did see the weather  warnings in place but do you think that  the  fire services and other emergency  services are equipped right now to be  able to deal with events like we've seen  i think up until yesterday morning i i'd  have said yes but we are still working  on the effects of climate change i think  yesterday was a game changer um we we're  certainly not immune to to the impacts  of climate change and certainly here in  west yorkshire you know we've been  planning because the modern fires that  we have up here we we run campaigns we  equip our staff we train our staff to  deal with what extended wildfires we  have floodings we're equipped and  trained to deal with them but yesterday  was really something very very different  it took  the moorland type of firefighting into a  suburban and an urban environment and i  have some shots that were shown from  london yesterday and the tragic  consequences down there fire getting  into an urban environment like it did  yesterday from an open field is  something that's really unique is the  source of only today really happens is  the likes of california and southern  europe  you say that yesterday was a game  changer what do you think does need to  get changed going forwards then  i think there's a number of things that  i think certainly was a fire rescue  service have to look at yesterday and we  have to learn from it i i was i think we  were very effective i think we can  always be better  i i i know people have different views  on this climate change is here to stay  days like yesterday will happen again  and again and again so we we we have to  design our urban our suburban  environments we have to modify our  behaviors and we have to accept that  working the way that we do living the  way that we do has consequences when we  have days like yesterday  and unfortunately the shots that i can  see on screen now that's the sort of  outcome that will happen if we don't  adapt to to climate change and its  effects of the sort of superheat that we  had yesterday  do you think you need more funding as  well  i i i think we'd always look for more  funding  everything more firefighters  more equipment always costs money it has  to come from somewhere um but but  equally just just to make a point as  well that yesterday was an extraordinary  day um there's almost  however many of our engines we could  have had yesterday we could have just  kept sending them to incidents so yes of  course there are funding pressures on  the fire rescue service  and firefighters as well um but let's  not lose sight of the fact that  yesterday was extraordinary and we need  to plan for the extraordinary going  forward  just looking at some of the pictures as  i know you are on the screen and it is  just extraordinary to see the  devastation that's happened  people's homes completely  devastated  what is the cleanup operation if you  like that is happening now to try and  help the people who've been affected  i i think certainly the the the images  that you've got on screen at the moment  um are clearly not based down in the  area but i don't expect the local  council local resilience forum would  step in the fire and rescue service and  other public sector agencies and indeed  voluntary sectors will be going in there  they'll be helping those people because  remember most those people maybe went to  work yesterday morning left the house  expecting to walk back yesterday  afternoon um but their that their their  clothes everything that they vital  medicines everything that they needed to  live their life is gone and turned to  ash so the level of support that will be  provided um from partners across a range  of um different different parts of  society will be absolutely vital to  those people just to get back on the  feet and then start to rebuild their  lives in their homes again  thank you so much for being on the  programme this morning this afternoon i  should say a really important voice  there dave alton  of the west yorkshire foreign rescue  service
 

